{
  // Output filename and BoardLoader data-variable name:
  //   boardName "RP2040" -> designer/boards/RP2040/RP2040.json -> const RP2040Data = {...}
  "boardName": "RP2040",

  // Fallback human-readable name shown in the designer UI.  Overridden
  // by the matching `<id>.name=` entry in a sibling-or-ancestor
  // boards.txt when one is present (matched via `<id>.build.variant=`
  // against this variant's directory name) — for this board that's
  // "iLabs Challenger 2040 LTE" (variants/rp2040/boards.txt).
  "displayName": "Raspberry Pi Pico (RP2040)",

  // Same parser dialect as every other RP2040 board — see
  // ../board.json for the full rationale.
  "family": "rp2040",

  "inputOnlyGpios": [],

  // Default transports — Serial only. This board's onboard cellular
  // modem (u-blox SARA-R410M, LTE-M/NB-IoT) is reached over a plain UART
  // (Serial2 / GPIO4-7) plus GPIO power/reset control lines — not an
  // IP-capable WiFi/BLE radio — so it doesn't qualify for the "rp2040w"
  // bucket's tcp/http/ble .ino templates (those target the native cyw43
  // WiFi.h stack). This board would need its own dedicated AT-command/
  // PPP modem .ino templates before it could genuinely offer tcp/http
  // connections. Plain "rp2040" (Serial only) is correct, same reasoning
  // already established for the challenger_2040_lora/subghz siblings'
  // onboard radio modules.
  "connections": ["serial"],

  "pinNotes": {},

  // pfodWeb NOTE (dedicated-hardware pin-exclusion + LED/NeoPixel tagging
  // audit, 2026-07-13):
  //
  // Board: iLabs "Challenger RP2040 LTE" / "Challenger RP2040 LTE MkII"
  // (Adafruit Feather form factor), combining an RP2040 with an onboard
  // u-blox SARA-R410M LTE-M/NB-IoT cellular modem module.
  //
  // Sources consulted:
  // 1. https://ilabs.se/challenger-rp2040-lte-datasheet/ (official iLabs
  //    datasheet, fetched directly) — states verbatim: "GPIO4 acts as
  //    UART1 TXD", "GPIO5 acts as UART1 RXD", "GPIO6 acts as UART1 CTS",
  //    "GPIO7 acts as UART1 RTS" (the SARA-R410M's UART link); "GPIO13
  //    is connected the power on signal of the SARA R410M module",
  //    "GPIO14 is connected to the reset signal of the SARA R410M
  //    module", "GPIO15 is connected to the LDO that supplies the modem
  //    with power". Also: "This LED is connected to pin D15 and can
  //    easily be controlled by the user program" (referring to iLabs'
  //    own board-silkscreen "Dxx" numbering, NOT arduino-pico's Dn
  //    aliases — see cross-reference note below on resolving this to a
  //    GPIO number). Does not state the LED's active-high/active-low
  //    polarity. No NeoPixel or discrete user pushbutton mentioned
  //    anywhere on the page.
  // 2. https://circuitpython.org/board/challenger_rp2040_lte/ and the
  //    matching CircuitPython pins.c for board "challenger_rp2040_lte"
  //    (github.com/adafruit/circuitpython, ports/raspberrypi/boards/
  //    challenger_rp2040_lte/pins.c, fetched directly) — independently
  //    confirms `board.LED` -> GPIO19, resolving the datasheet's
  //    ambiguous "pin D15" silkscreen reference to an unambiguous GPIO
  //    number that matches this board's own local pins_arduino.h
  //    (PIN_LED=(19u), D17=(19u), LED_BUILTIN=PIN_LED) exactly. The same
  //    CircuitPython pins.c defines no NEOPIXEL and no BUTTON pin for
  //    this board — corroborating the datasheet's silence on those.
  // 3. https://github.com/earlephilhower/arduino-pico
  //    variants/challenger_2040_lte/pins_arduino.h (upstream arduino-pico
  //    source that this repo's local copy is derived from, fetched
  //    directly) — confirmed byte-identical pin assignments to the local
  //    copy in this repo (PIN_LED=19, Serial2 TX/RX/CTS/RTS=4/5/6/7,
  //    PIN_SARA_ON=13, PIN_SARA_RST=14, PIN_SARA_PWR=15); no NEOPIXEL or
  //    button define present in the upstream file either.
  // 4. https://ilabs.se/challenger-rp2040-wifi-datasheet/ (sibling board
  //    in the same iLabs Challenger RP2040 product line, already audited
  //    in this repo — see challenger_2040_wifi/board.json) — explicitly
  //    states "Active level: High" for its own onboard user LED, the
  //    same shared reference LED circuit (GPIO -> resistor -> LED ->
  //    GND) reused across this entire product line. Combined with every
  //    other RP2040 board audited in this family to date (rpipico,
  //    wiznet_5500_evb_pico, adafruit_feather_scorpio, challenger_2040_
  //    lora, challenger_2040_wifi, challenger_2040_nfc, challenger_2040_
  //    subghz — all confirmed or cross-reference-inferred active-high,
  //    none active-low), GPIO19 is tagged led_high here on the same
  //    cross-referenced basis used for the challenger_2040_nfc/subghz
  //    siblings.
  // 5. Local codebase: this board's own pins_arduino.h
  //    (variants/rp2040/rp2040/challenger_2040_lte/pins_arduino.h) —
  //    already carries a prior-session "pfodWeb NOTE" (lines 66-68)
  //    recording that D14-D16 were deliberately NOT declared as aliases
  //    for GPIO13/14/15 (the SARA-R4XX's ON/RST/PWR control lines), and
  //    that GPIO4-7 (the modem's Serial2/CTS/RTS UART) were likewise
  //    never given D-number aliases in the first place. Verified
  //    directly: the only `static const uint8_t` declarations are
  //    D0-D13, D17, and A0-A5, resolving to GPIOs {16,17,24,23,22,2,3,0,
  //    1,12,11,10,9,8,19,29,28,27,26,25,21} — none of {4,5,6,7,13,14,15}
  //    appear anywhere. This already fully excludes all seven
  //    SARA-R410M-dedicated GPIOs from the Designer's pin list with zero
  //    board.json involvement.
  // 6. pfodWeb_src/build_boards.js (read directly, not executed, to
  //    confirm parser behavior — see "WHY NO chipGpios ENTRIES..." below
  //    for the exact mechanism traced, including line numbers).
  //
  // Full findings/sources trail:
  // boardsDetails/rp2040/rp2040/challenger_2040_lte/notes.txt
  //
  // WHY NO chipGpios ENTRIES FOR THE MODEM PINS (GPIO4,5,6,7,13,14,15):
  // Same reasoning independently verified this session and already
  // documented on the challenger_2040_lora/challenger_2040_subghz
  // siblings: build_boards.js's buildEsp32Board() only lists a GPIO in
  // the Designer's pin picker if it appears either (a) as a `static
  // const uint8_t` alias in pins_arduino.h, or (b) as a key in this
  // file's chipGpios object (`allGpios = new Set(aliasesByGpio.keys());
  // for (const key of Object.keys(chipGpiosMap)) allGpios.add(n);` —
  // build_boards.js ~line 535-539). Since none of GPIO4,5,6,7,13,14,15
  // carry a pins_arduino.h alias (source #5 above), they are already
  // 100% absent from the Designer's pin list with no board.json changes
  // required. Adding chipGpios entries for them would be
  // COUNTERPRODUCTIVE: build_boards.js unconditionally appends a "Check
  // if available on board" note to ANY rp2040-family GPIO absent from
  // pins_arduino.h, regardless of whether a chipGpios entry (with its
  // own custom note) exists for it (`if (!inPinsH && cfg.family !==
  // 'esp32' && cfg.family !== 'esp8266') noteParts.push('Check if
  // available on board');` — build_boards.js ~line 718, confirmed by
  // direct read this session). Adding entries here would re-add each
  // pin to allGpios as a bare, visible "GPIO<n>" row carrying that
  // redundant/misleading note alongside the real explanation — undoing
  // the full concealment the pins_arduino.h omission already achieves.
  // (NOTE: the earlier challenger_2040_wifi and challenger_2040_nfc
  // sibling audits this session DID add chipGpios entries for some of
  // their own unaliased dedicated pins, e.g. wifi's GPIO4/5/13/19 and
  // nfc's GPIO9/10/11/12/14 — this appears to be an inconsistency
  // between those two earlier audits and the later lora/subghz
  // audits' more carefully-traced reasoning, not a deliberate
  // alternative approach. Flagging for awareness only; fixing those
  // other boards is out of scope for this task.)
  //
  // Also confirmed: no onboard NeoPixel and no onboard user button
  // (beyond the standard RP2040 BOOTSEL, not a normal GPIO, out of
  // scope per the rpipico/board.json precedent) on this board — neither
  // the iLabs datasheet nor the CircuitPython pins.c mention either.
  //
  // Per-pin reasoning:
  //   GPIO4  - PIN_SERIAL2_TX - SARA-R410M modem's dedicated UART1 TX
  //            line ("GPIO4 acts as UART1 TXD"). Not general-purpose.
  //            Already absent from pins_arduino.h aliases; no chipGpios
  //            entry added (see "WHY NO chipGpios ENTRIES..." above).
  //   GPIO5  - PIN_SERIAL2_RX - SARA-R410M modem's dedicated UART1 RX
  //            line. Same reasoning as GPIO4.
  //   GPIO6  - PIN_SERIAL2_CTS - SARA-R410M modem's UART1 hardware
  //            flow-control CTS line. Same reasoning as GPIO4.
  //   GPIO7  - PIN_SERIAL2_RTS - SARA-R410M modem's UART1 hardware
  //            flow-control RTS line. Same reasoning as GPIO4.
  //   GPIO13 - PIN_SARA_ON - SARA-R410M modem's power-on control line,
  //            driven by the RP2040. Not general-purpose. Same reasoning
  //            as GPIO4.
  //   GPIO14 - PIN_SARA_RST - SARA-R410M modem's reset control line,
  //            driven by the RP2040. Same reasoning as GPIO4.
  //   GPIO15 - PIN_SARA_PWR - SARA-R410M modem's power-supply LDO
  //            enable line, driven by the RP2040. Same reasoning as
  //            GPIO4.
  //   GPIO19 - PIN_LED / LED_BUILTIN (D17 alias) - onboard general-
  //            purpose user LED. Presence and GPIO number confirmed via
  //            the official iLabs datasheet ("pin D15" in iLabs' own
  //            silkscreen numbering, cross-referenced to GPIO19 via
  //            CircuitPython's independent board.LED=GPIO19 definition,
  //            matching this board's own PIN_LED=(19u)). Active-high
  //            polarity is a cross-referenced inference: this board's
  //            own datasheet does not state the active level, but the
  //            sibling challenger_2040_wifi board's datasheet explicitly
  //            states "Active level: High" for the identical shared
  //            reference LED circuit reused across this iLabs product
  //            line, and every other RP2040 board audited in this
  //            family to date has been active-high. digital_input
  //            dropped to match this repo's established led_high
  //            precedent for dedicated onboard LED pins; pwm_output kept
  //            since arduino-pico's analogWrite() works on any RP2040
  //            GPIO, enabling brightness dimming.
  //
  // GPIO19 carries no SPI/I2C/other bus alias on this board (SPI0 lives
  // on GPIO20-23, Wire0 on GPIO0-1), so the build_boards.js unconditional
  // bus-capability auto-append (the "confirmed button/LED pin also
  // aliased as SDA/SCL/SS/MOSI/MISO/SCK" bug class flagged for this
  // audit) does not apply here — no pins_arduino.h edit was needed.
  //
  // Also noted (out of scope for this audit): a GitHub CircuitPython
  // issue (adafruit/circuitpython#8272) independently confirms GPIO20
  // (silkscreened "SDI") is a genuine general-purpose SPI0 header pin
  // (PIN_SPI0_MISO) that is simply missing a `static const uint8_t`
  // alias in both this board's local pins_arduino.h and upstream
  // arduino-pico/CircuitPython — a pre-existing pins_arduino.h gap
  // unrelated to the modem/LED audit (GPIO20 is not dedicated hardware,
  // just unaliased), flagged here only for awareness of a possible
  // future non-audit fix, matching the analogous gap already flagged on
  // the challenger_2040_lora sibling's notes.txt.
  "chipGpios": {
    "19": { "capabilities": ["digital_output", "pwm_output", "led_high"], "note": "PIN_LED / LED_BUILTIN (D17 alias) — onboard general-purpose user LED. Presence confirmed via the official iLabs datasheet (\"pin D15\" in iLabs' own silkscreen numbering) cross-referenced to GPIO19 via CircuitPython's independent board.LED=GPIO19 definition, matching this board's own PIN_LED=(19u). Active-high polarity is a cross-referenced inference from the sibling challenger_2040_wifi board's datasheet (\"Active level: High\" for the identical shared reference LED circuit reused across this iLabs product line) plus every other RP2040 board audited in this family to date being active-high." }
  },

  // Same 8-bit analogWrite() default as every other RP2040/RP2350 board.
  "pwm": { "min": 0, "max": 255, "method": "analogWrite" },

  // No hardware DAC on RP2040/RP2350.
  "dac": {},

  // RP2040's one 12-bit ADC (0-4095), ~3.3V reference — same as every
  // other RP2040 board. Matches this board's own pins_arduino.h
  // (NUM_ANALOG_INPUTS=4, ADC_RESOLUTION=12u).
  "adc": { "bits": 12, "max": 4095, "defaultRefVolts": "3.3" }
}
